#let assignments = [
  1. In-class participation
  
  Students are expected to come to class, participate in discussions, and ask questions. Many of the lectures will follow the other assignments so materials covered in class will help students develop their own projects.

  2. Annotated bibliographies, develop research questions/hypotheses, and find guiding theory

  Students will be expected to read literature on their topic to see what has been published and what can be future directions for the project area. From the literature review, students will be expected to create research questions and/or hypotheses about their project. Additionally, students should have a guiding theory guiding their research project. 

  3. Advisor check in

  Students without advisors will look up potential advisors. This assignment contains several sections, including: 
    
    - How to reach out to potential advisors?
    - How to use meeting times effectively?
    - What to bring to advisor meetings?
    - How to communicate with your advisor?

  and will end with students getting their research questions and/or hypotheses approved by their advisor.

  4. Create a timeline for Data H195A & H195B

  Students will develop a weekly timeline of what they will accomplish for the fall and spring semester regarding their project. The timeline will include requirements for the semester, such as reading literature, creating research questions/hypotheses, cleaning data, writing, data documentation. Students will then sign the document and get their advisor's signature to indicate both know what is needed to complete the project. 

  5. Preliminary proposal

  Students will submit their preliminary proposal, including feedback from the instructor from previous assignments. Students will also report reflections on the research process for their projects.

  6. Methodology proposal

  Students should discuss the methodology proposed for their research project. This information will include:

    - How the data was collected
    - Details about the dataset
    - Details about the sample
    - How the methods fit within each student's domain emphasis
    - Details about the measure(s) used (e.g., reliability, validity)
    - Details about missing data and how it will be handled
    - Detailed description about the analytic approach

  7. Create a GitHub/MaxQDA/Berkeley Box repo

  For students working with quantitative data, the reproducibility workflow will be set up with a GitHub repo, versions of datasets, data documentation (i.e., codebook), and output files. Documentation of code and the research process will be established as a set of best practices to be followed once the research project is underway. This framework for sharing code and results with a project partner or advisor will follow a rubric.

  For students working with qualitative data and corresponding analyses, the workflow will include setting up a Berkeley Box account to hold qualitative data (i.e., interview/focus group/etc.) and documentation about organization of participant data, as well as documentation on qualitative coding. This framework for sharing results with a project partner or advisor will follow a rubric.

  8. Presenting project in group setting

  Students will present and defend their project proposal in a group setting. Peer students will offer feedback and identify clarifications.

  9. Final proposal

  The culmination of all these efforts will be a paper that summarizes and includes the information from the previous steps, and presents a tight proposal for the research project.  Accompanying the paper will be a slide deck for the oral presentation  and a github repository for the project work to be managed through. Students will identify a project mentor and/or a project partner where appropriate.  
]

#let gen_ai_policy = [
  As a matter of general policy, *AI is ALLOWED* in the following contexts:
  
    - Brainstorming and idea generation
      - Students may use AI tools to generate initial ideas or questions
        - Students will document prompts used when using AI tools
    
    - Coding assistance
      - Students may use AI tools to debug code or understand code syntax
        - Students will document prompts for debugging code/understanding code syntax

    - Grammar and style checking
      - Students may use AI tools for grammar checks and style checking
        - Students will document prompts for grammar checks and style checking
      
  *AI is PROHIBITED* in the following contexts:

    - Generating sentences, paragraphs, or entire assignments
      - Any and all writing must be completed by the student

    - Summarizing any reading, assignment, or instructions
      - Students must read and engage with the course materials yourself
]

